    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "The $\\tan x$ and $\\cot x$ terms in the function are periodic, so the tangent function, $f(x) = \\tan x$, and its reflection $g(x) = \\cot x$, undergo in-phase repetitions, meaning $y$'s period is a half of that of $f(x) = |g(x)| = |\\cot x|$'s period.\nTo solve for the period of $f(x) = \\tan x$ and $g(x) = \\cot x$, we consider a point of the function, say $x$, and calculate the increment required for its repetition.\nFrom the unit circle, we see that $\\tan^{-1}(1) = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$, thus $\\tan \\frac{\\pi}{4} = 1$.\nSimilarly, $\\cot^{-1}(1) = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$, and $\\cot(\\frac{\\pi}{4}) = 1$.\nThe fact, therefore, that $2 \\cdot \\frac{\\pi}{4} = \\pi$ represents a $\\pi$-periodic increment in the function is obvious. [8]\nHence, $\\boxed{\\pi/2}$ is the period of the given function.",
